+- Precision issues may arise if `ibuf_size` calculation does not account for padding or alignment requirements of the underlying MPI implementation, though `char` type minimizes this risk [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### MPI Master-Slave Octree Volume Rendering
-A variant where slaves traverse an Octree (OTree) structure to find empty/non-empty blocks, computing ray segments only for non-empty blocks to optimize rendering [S1, unofficial]. The master sends volume metadata (bmin, bmax, mmu_ratio, file path) and transfer function data [S1, unofficial]. Slaves perform ray casting on these optimized segments [S1, unofficial]. This differs from standard voxel-based ray casting by leveraging hierarchical data structures for early ray termination and empty space skipping [S1, unofficial].
+### [MPI Master-Slave Octree Volume Rendering](2744-mpi-master-slave-octree-volume-rendering.md)
+Uses spatial octree encoding to partition hexahedral elements, allowing efficient traversal of empty space and one-time distribution of the octree subtree structure.
+
+### [MPI Parallel Slice-Based Volume Rendering](3827-mpi-parallel-slice-based-volume-rendering.md)
+Decomposes the volume along the slicing axis (typically Z) among processes, handling domain decomposition and image concatenation within the rendering loop itself, operating on uniform slice blocks rather than hierarchical structures.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Dominated by ray casting on slaves and MPI communication [S1, unofficial]. Image assembly is $O(\text{height} \times \text{width})$ [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(\text{slave\_size} \times \text{height} \times \text{width} \times 3)$ for image buffers on the master [S1, unofficial]. Slaves store volume data and transfer functions [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Timing is measured over multiple frames, averaging out startup costs [S1, unofficial]. The use of non-blocking MPI receives allows for potential overlap, though the example waits for all before assembly [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simple master-slave model, leverages MPI for process management, TCP for flexible image transfer [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Master becomes a bottleneck for image assembly and control [S1, unofficial]. Round-robin row distribution may lead to load imbalance if ray casting cost varies significantly per row (e.g., due to opacity variations) [S1, unofficial]. Fixed buffer sizes for TF and messages limit flexibility [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** Suitable for static volumes where load balancing is not critical or where the volume is uniform [S1, unofficial]. Not ideal for highly heterogeneous volumes without additional load balancing strategies [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** The master node's image assembly is $O(\text{height} \times \text{width})$ for copying pixels. The rendering time is dominated by the slowest slave process.
+- **Memory Complexity:** The master node requires $O(\text{slave\_size} \times \lceil \text{height}/\text{slave\_size} \rceil \times \text{width} \times 3)$ bytes for the receive buffers, plus $O(\text{height} \times \text{width} \times 3)$ for the final image. This is roughly $O(\text{height} \times \text{width} \times 3)$, effectively doubling the memory requirement for the image buffer on the master compared to a single-process render [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Simple master-slave abstraction; explicit control over parameter distribution; handles uneven row distributions gracefully [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** The master node becomes a bottleneck for image assembly and control message broadcasting; TCP/MPI hybrid communication may introduce overhead; not given in the sources for specific hardware performance metrics.
 
 ## Background
-This workflow originates from distributed volume rendering systems using MPI for process coordination and TCP for data transfer [S1, unofficial]. It is commonly found in academic and research implementations of parallel volume rendering, particularly those focusing on master-slave architectures for simplicity and ease of implementation [S1, unofficial].
+This method originates from an unofficial GitHub repository `hopexn/VolumeRenderingParallel` [S1, unofficial]. It is designed to coordinate parallel volume rendering by spawning slave processes via MPI, distributing transfer functions, and assembling the final image from per-slice data [S1, unofficial].
 
